WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:06.960
Proposition forty-four sets a spending rule for private nonprofit

2
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.140
safety-net clinics.

3
00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.840
These clinics serve many low-income and uninsured patients.

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.360
The proposal does not apply the same rule to

5
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.840
every doctor, hospital, or publicly operated clinic.

6
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.120
Covered clinics would have to spend at least ninety

7
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.200
percent of annual revenue on health care services, leaving

8
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.340
no more than ten percent for other expenses.

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.680
The attorney general would define the categories in more

10
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:31.000
detail.

11
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.320
Imagine a clinic manager planning next year’s budget.

12
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.240
For a hypothetical clinic receiving ten million dollars, the

13
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.640
ninety percent minimum is nine million dollars.

14
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.520
If qualifying spending is eight million, the shortfall is

15
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:46.160
one million.

16
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.760
This is arithmetic to explain the rule, not a

17
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:50.720
real clinic’s budget.

18
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.500
A nurse depends on both care resources and working

19
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.060
systems.

20
00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.940
A clinic falling short would owe a penalty equal

21
00:00:58.940 --> 00:00:59.660
to the shortfall.

22
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.620
It could recover that money by complying within five

23
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:02.980
years.

24
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.700
Temporary waivers would be available in some circumstances.

25
00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.900
The rule therefore has enforcement mechanisms as well as

26
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:11.440
a target.

27
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.860
Which costs qualify matters.

28
00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.910
An administrative label is not proof that an expense

29
00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:21.670
is wasteful; scheduling, records, and other systems can support

30
00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:21.950
care.

31
00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.330
Final definitions affect how clinics would change their budgets.

32
00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:28.590
The analyst estimates enforcement costs in the low tens

33
00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.670
of millions of dollars each year, funded by fees

34
00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:33.410
on affected clinics.

35
00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.850
Other public costs are uncertain and depend on how

36
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.330
clinics respond, including whether some reduce operations or close.

37
00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:44.370
A voter might want a larger share of clinic

38
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:48.010
revenue devoted to services for patients, with reporting and

39
00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:49.710
consequences for falling short.

40
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.010
They may believe a clear minimum is a useful

41
00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.370
accountability tool.

42
00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:59.090
Reaching a ratio, however, does not by itself measure

43
00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:00.510
quality or outcomes.

44
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.150
You might ask whether one ratio fits different clinics.

45
00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.990
Another voter could worry that a rigid limit constrains

46
00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:12.230
necessary support costs, adds fees, or makes some clinics

47
00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.290
financially fragile.

48
00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:17.010
A clinic can improve its reported ratio in different

49
00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:20.950
ways; the important outcome is access to effective care,

50
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.650
not only the percentage on a report.

51
00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.170
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 34

52
00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:30.810
percent said yes, 61 percent no, and 6 percent

53
00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:31.310
were unsure.

54
00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.810
These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.

55
00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:40.770
Yes adopts the new spending rule; no leaves existing

56
00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.250
reporting and spending requirements in place.

57
00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:47.390
Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links,

58
00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:48.970
and official voter guide.

59
00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:52.850
Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice.
